.TH DINGDONGDITCH 1 2026-11-01 "DingDongDitch 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dingdongditch \- find open ports on hosts you own, and work out what is behind them
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dingdongditch scan
.RI [ options ]
.I target
.RI [ target ...]
.br
.B dingdongditch analyze
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I report
.br
.B dingdongditch update\-db
.RB [ \-\-db
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-\-full ]
.RB [ \-\-epss\-date
.IR YYYY-MM-DD ]
.br
.B dingdongditch train
.RB [ \-\-db
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-\-model
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-\-fast ]
.RB [ \-\-test
.IR YYYY-MM-DD ]
.br
.B dingdongditch
.RB [ \-V | \-\-version ]
.RB [ \-\-help ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dingdongditch
knocks on the TCP ports of a host, asks each open port what it is, and reports
the service, product and version it found.
With
.B \-\-karen\-mode
it also reads web pages and TLS certificates, guesses the operating system,
and proves common misconfigurations, which it marks
.BR CONFIRMED .
.PP
Services that only answer in their own protocol get one read-only question in
it.
DNS servers are asked for
.BR version.bind ,
and PostgreSQL is sent a startup message for a protocol version that does not
exist, which it refuses before any login.
.PP
It detects and never exploits.
Only scan systems you own or have written permission to test.
.PP
Scans don't use the risk model yet, so the Risk column says
.I model not trained yet
unless a finding is
.BR CONFIRMED .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B scan
Scan one or more targets and print what was found.
.TP
.B analyze
Read an nmap XML report made with
.B nmap \-sV \-oX
and print it the same way.
Give
.B \-
as
.I report
to read from standard input.
.TP
.B update\-db
Download the public vulnerability data into a local database, or bring it up
to date.
See
.BR DATABASE .
.TP
.B train
Train the model that ranks CVEs by how likely they are to be exploited, or test
how well it would have done in the past.
See
.BR "RISK MODEL" .
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Scan options
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-engine " " \fIengine\fR
Which scanner to use:
.B own
(the built-in scanner, the default) or
.B nmap
(needs nmap installed).
.TP
.BR \-P ", " \-\-profile " " \fIprofile\fR
How many ports to scan:
.BR quick ,
.B default
or
.BR full .
See
.BR PORTS .
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-ports " " \fIports\fR
Scan exactly these ports, for example
.B 22,80,443
or
.B 8000\-9000
or both together.
This wins over
.B \-\-profile
and over the all-ports default of
.BR \-\-karen\-mode .
.TP
.B \-\-karen\-mode
Demand to speak to every port's manager.
See
.BR "KAREN MODE" .
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-timeout " " \fIseconds\fR
Built-in scanner only.
How long to wait for each port to accept a connection.
Default 1.0, minimum 0.05.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-concurrency " " \fIcount\fR
Built-in scanner only.
How many ports to try at once.
Default 500, minimum 1.
Lowered automatically if the system runs out of sockets.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-save " " \fIfile\fR
Also save the raw results to
.IR file :
JSON with the built-in scanner, nmap XML with the nmap engine.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print JSON instead of a table.
Only the JSON goes to standard output, so it can be piped.
Also accepted by
.BR analyze .
.SS update\-db options
.TP
.B \-\-db \fIfile\fR
Use this database file instead of the default.
See
.BR DATABASE .
.TP
.B \-\-full
Check every year of NVD data for changes, not just the last week's.
Without it, a run within a week of the last one only reads NVD's list of
recent changes.
.TP
.B \-\-epss\-date \fIYYYY-MM-DD\fR
Also save the EPSS scores from this past day, so a model can be compared with
EPSS as it was before it knew what happened next.
Can be given more than once.
Scores go back to April 2021.
.SS train options
.B \-\-db
works as for
.BR update\-db .
.TP
.B \-\-model \fIfile\fR
Save the model here instead of
.I model.joblib
next to the database.
.TP
.B \-\-fast
Use logistic regression instead of XGBoost: a few minutes instead of about 15,
and a little less sharp.
.TP
.B \-\-test \fIYYYY-MM-DD\fR
Save nothing.
Train on only what was known on that day, then print how well the model, CVSS,
public exploits and EPSS from that day found the CVEs CISA listed in the
following year.
The year must be over.
.SS General options
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Show help and exit.
Works after a command too.
.SH TARGETS
A target can be an IP address, a hostname or a subnet in CIDR form.
Separate several with spaces or commas.
Duplicates are removed.
A subnet can have at most 65,536 addresses (a /16), so a typo cannot start a
huge scan.
.SH PORTS
.TP
.B quick
107 hand-picked common and risky ports.
.TP
.B default
Ports 1\-1024 plus the quick list, 1,094 ports in all.
.TP
.B full
All 65,535 TCP ports.
.PP
With
.BR "\-\-engine nmap" ,
.B quick
and
.B default
are nmap's own top 100 and top 1000 ports instead.
.SH KAREN MODE
.B \-\-karen\-mode
scans all 65,535 ports unless
.B \-p
is given.
For each open port it also:
.IP \(bu 2
tries more probes on ports the first probe did not identify, over plain text
and TLS
.IP \(bu 2
fetches the home page of web ports for the title, telling headers and
application
.IP \(bu 2
reads TLS certificates and flags ones that are expired, not yet valid,
self-signed or weakly signed, and old TLS versions
.IP \(bu 2
guesses each host's operating system from clues in the banners, and shows
which port gave each clue
.IP \(bu 2
registers with IRC servers as a visitor, joining no channels, and opens an
anonymous SMB1 session with no user or password, to read the software's name and
version.
If an IRC server refuses for connecting too often, it waits about a minute and
tries once more.
.IP \(bu 2
runs the checks in
.B CONFIRMED CHECKS
.PP
The operating system guess is only a guess.
Banners can be wrong or changed by the owner.
.PP
With
.BR "\-\-engine nmap" ,
this flag only scans all ports and uses every nmap version probe.
The extras above come from the built-in scanner.
.SH CONFIRMED CHECKS
Each check does the smallest exchange that proves the problem, then leaves.
Nothing is guessed, listed, read or changed.
.TP
.B ftp\-anonymous
Anyone can log in to the FTP server.
Logs in as the public
.B anonymous
user and quits straight away.
.TP
.B open\-shell
A port gives a command prompt with no login.
Recognised from the prompt it sends.
Nothing is ever typed into it.
.TP
.B redis\-no\-password
Redis answers without a password.
Uses the read-only
.B INFO server
reply that banner grabbing already asked for.
.TP
.B mongodb\-no\-auth
MongoDB answers without a login.
Asks for the list of databases, filtered to a made-up name so that no real
names come back.
.TP
.B telnet\-open
Telnet is running, which sends everything unencrypted.
Proved by its greeting, without logging in.
.SH OUTPUT
The table goes to standard output.
Warnings and progress go to standard error.
.PP
.B \-\-json
prints a list with one object per open port.
Its fields are
.BR host ,
.BR port ,
.BR protocol ,
.BR state ,
.BR name ,
.BR product ,
.BR version ,
.BR extrainfo ,
.BR cpes ,
.BR banner ,
.BR tls ,
.BR http_title ,
.BR http_headers ,
.BR tls_cert ,
.BR os_guess ,
.B os_evidence
and
.BR findings .
Each finding has
.BR check ,
.BR title ,
.BR why ,
.B fix
and
.BR status .
.SH MISSING PORTS
Going too fast can make open ports look closed, especially inside a virtual
machine or over a slow link.
If a scan finds fewer ports than expected, slow it down:
.RS
.PP
.B dingdongditch scan
.I target
.B \-c 100 \-t 3
.RE
.PP
Ports that time out get a second, slower try.
This is skipped when more than 5,000 ports time out, which is normal behind a
firewall, and the scan prints a warning.
.PP
Silent FTP and mail ports are given up to 12 seconds to greet, because some
servers delay their greeting.
.SH DATABASE
.B update\-db
downloads four free public sources into one SQLite file:
.TP
.B NVD
Every CVE, with its description, CVSS scores, weakness types (CWE) and the
products and versions it affects, from the yearly JSON feeds of the US National
Vulnerability Database.
.TP
.B CISA KEV
The CVEs that attackers are known to have really exploited.
.TP
.B Exploit\-DB
Which CVEs have public exploit code.
Only the index is downloaded, never the code itself.
.TP
.B EPSS
FIRST.org's daily estimate of how likely each CVE is to be exploited in the next
30 days, and with
.BR \-\-epss\-date ,
its estimates from chosen past days.
.PP
The first run downloads about 260 MB, takes 10 to 15 minutes, and makes a file
of about 750 MB.
Later runs only fetch what changed.
Each source is saved in one step, so if a download fails, that source keeps its
old data and the others are still updated.
.PP
The file is
.I vulns.db
in a
.I dingdongditch
folder in
.B $XDG_DATA_HOME
(normally
.IR ~/.local/share )
on Linux,
.I ~/Library/Application Support
on macOS and
.B %LOCALAPPDATA%
on Windows.
Set
.B DINGDONGDITCH_DB
to a file name, or use
.BR \-\-db ,
to put it somewhere else.
.SH RISK MODEL
.B train
teaches a model which CVEs get exploited, using the CVEs on CISA's KEV list as
the examples of real attacks.
It looks at the CVSS details, the weakness type, the vendor and product, words
in the description, public exploits and the CVE's age.
References are left out, because they pile up after an attack and would give
the answer away.
.PP
.B \-\-test
checks it fairly, as if it were a past day: the model, CVSS and EPSS (as it was
that day) each rank every CVE that was public and not yet on CISA's list, and
the report shows how many of the CVEs CISA listed in the following year each
would have caught by fixing the top 1%, 5% or 10% of its ranking.
.PP
The settings in use were chosen the same way, on the year before May 2025.
.SH LIMITATIONS
.IP \(bu 2
TCP only, so UDP services such as SNMP are not seen.
.IP \(bu 2
It makes full TCP connections, which the target can log.
.IP \(bu 2
Many services that only answer in their own protocol, such as java\-rmi,
are named from their port number with no version.
.IP \(bu 2
PostgreSQL never states its version before login.
It gets a range of releases instead, from the source line its error came from.
.IP \(bu 2
SMB versions need Karen mode and SMB1, which newer servers turn off.
.IP \(bu 2
Versions come from what a service says about itself, and Linux distributions
often patch old versions without changing the number.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including a scan that found no open ports.
.TP
.B 1
The scan failed, a file was not found or is not an nmap report, the database
could not be opened, a data source could not be downloaded, or the model could
not be trained or tested.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was wrong.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B dingdongditch scan 192.168.195.130
Scan the default ports of one host.
.TP
.B dingdongditch scan 192.168.195.130 \-p 8000\-9000
Scan a range of ports.
.TP
.B dingdongditch scan 192.168.195.0/24 \-P quick
Scan a whole subnet quickly.
.TP
.B dingdongditch scan 10.0.0.1,10.0.0.2
Scan two hosts.
.TP
.B dingdongditch scan 192.168.195.130 \-\-karen\-mode
Scan every port and run every safe check.
.TP
.B dingdongditch scan 192.168.195.130 \-\-json \-o scan.json
Print JSON and save it to a file.
.TP
.B dingdongditch scan 192.168.195.130 \-e nmap \-o scan.xml
Scan with nmap and keep its XML report.
.TP
.B dingdongditch analyze scan.xml
Read that report later.
.TP
.B nmap \-sV \-oX \- 192.168.195.130 | dingdongditch analyze \-
Read a report straight from nmap.
.TP
.B dingdongditch update\-db
Download the vulnerability data, or bring it up to date.
.TP
.B dingdongditch update\-db \-\-db ~/lab/vulns.db \-\-full
Use another database file and check every year of NVD data.
.TP
.B dingdongditch update\-db \-\-epss\-date 2025\-05\-01
Also keep the EPSS scores from 1 May 2025.
.TP
.B dingdongditch train
Train the model on everything known today and save it.
.TP
.B dingdongditch train \-\-test 2025\-05\-01 \-\-fast
See how a quickly trained model would have done from 1 May 2025.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DINGDONGDITCH_DB
The database file to use when
.B \-\-db
is not given.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR nmap (1),
.BR sqlite3 (1)
